# Keyless reference for every variable this repo reads. Real values never live in a
# plaintext file — they go into the per-package `.env.local.enc`, encrypted by dotenvx.
#
#   bun install
#   cp .env.example apps/web/.env.personal    # fill in, stays git-ignored
#   cp .env.example packages/db/.env.personal
#
# `.env.personal` is a plaintext local-only overlay that layers on top of (and
# overrides) the encrypted file. Nothing else reads it and it is never committed.
#
# To put a value into the committed encrypted file instead — the normal path for
# anything a teammate also needs — add the key manually at the right line in the
# target `.env.local.enc`, then:
#
#   dotenvx set MY_VAR "value" -f apps/web/.env.local.enc
#
# Order matters: `dotenvx set` on a key that does not exist yet appends it to the
# bottom, breaking the line-alignment the files rely on. Encrypting needs only the
# public key in the file header, so you can do this without the private key.
#
# Which file owns which variable:
#
#   apps/web/.env.local.enc      DATABASE_URL, SENTRY_DSN, NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN
#   apps/api/.env.local.enc      DATABASE_URL, PORT, CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, SENTRY_DSN
#   packages/db/.env.local.enc   DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL  (Prisma CLI only)
#
# Decryption keys live in a git-ignored `.env.keys` next to each one. New checkout:
# ask for those three keys, or re-encrypt with your own.

# ─── Neon Postgres ────────────────────────────────
# sslmode=verify-full, not require: `pg` treats them identically today, but a future
# major adopts libpq semantics where `require` skips certificate verification. Neon
# presents a publicly trusted cert, so verify-full costs nothing and can't silently weaken.

# Pooled (PgBouncer) — what the apps run on.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=verify-full"

# Direct — Prisma CLI only, so it belongs in packages/db. Migrate needs session-level
# advisory locks, which the pooler drops.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:password@host.region.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=verify-full"

# ─── API (apps/api) ───────────────────────────────
# Validated by apps/api/src/config/env/validation.ts — key order there mirrors the
# order here and in apps/api/.env.local.enc.
PORT="8080"
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="http://localhost:3000"

# ─── Sentry (apps/web and apps/api) ───────────────
# Not secrets — a DSN only grants event ingest. Leave them unset and the SDK no-ops,
# which is what you want for a throwaway checkout.
#
# Both apps report to the same Sentry project, so the DSN is identical in each
# `.env.local.enc`. NEXT_PUBLIC_ is web-only: it is inlined into the browser bundle,
# while the bare one is read at runtime by the Next server/edge configs and, in the
# API, by src/instrument.ts. The API deliberately reads it from process.env rather
# than EnvService — instrument.ts runs before the Nest container exists.
SENTRY_DSN="https://<key>@<org-id>.ingest.us.sentry.io/<project-id>"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN="https://<key>@<org-id>.ingest.us.sentry.io/<project-id>"

# Build-time only, and a real secret — uploads source maps so production stack traces
# aren't minified. Set in Vercel, not locally, which is why it is in no `.enc` file.
# Generate at: https://comp-ai-trial.sentry.io/settings/auth-tokens/ (scope: project:releases)
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=""

# NODE_ENV is deliberately absent. Next.js and the runtime own it; pinning it in a
# dotenv file is how a build ends up prerendering in the wrong mode.
